**Design.** JavaScript is single-threaded, so concurrency means limiting the number of in-flight promises. Keep a FIFO `pending` queue and a `running` counter. Whenever a task is added or finishes, call `drain()`, which starts tasks while `running < K`.

    type Task<T> = {
      id: string;
      run: () => Promise<T>;
      onSuccess?: (r: T, id: string) => void;
      onError?: (e: unknown, id: string) => void;
    };
    type Executor = <T>(t: Task<T>) => Promise<T>;

    class TaskQueue {
      private pending: Task<any>[] = [];
      private running = 0;
      constructor(
        private k: number,
        private executor: Executor = t => t.run()
      ) {}

      setExecutor(e: Executor) { this.executor = e; }

      add<T>(task: Task<T>) {
        this.pending.push(task);
        this.drain();
      }

      private drain() {
        while (this.running < this.k && this.pending.length) {
          const t = this.pending.shift()!;
          this.running++;
          this.executor(t)
            .then(r => t.onSuccess?.(r, t.id))
            .catch(e => t.onError?.(e, t.id))
            .finally(() => { this.running--; this.drain(); });
        }
      }
    }

**Why it works.** `running` is incremented synchronously before a task starts and decremented in `finally`, so a failing task still frees its slot. Each completion triggers `drain()` to fill free slots, so at most K run at once and queued tasks start in order.

**Pluggable executor.** The executor wraps `t.run`: a logging executor logs start and end; a retry executor loops up to N times with backoff; a rate-limiting executor awaits a token before running. Compose them as decorators.

**Edge cases.** Duplicate IDs (reject or track in a Set); exceptions thrown synchronously inside `run` (wrap in `Promise.resolve().then(...)`); callback errors shouldn't break the queue; K must be >= 1.

**Extensions.** `onIdle()` promise, `pause/resume`, priorities (heap instead of array, since `shift()` is O(n) for big arrays; use a linked list or index pointer), cancellation via `AbortSignal`, and a bounded queue with back-pressure.
